The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
err = sk_wait_data(sk, &timeo, NULL);
```
and the while loop in `mptcp_recvmsg`:
```c
while (copied < len) {
    ...
    err = sk_wait_data(sk, &timeo, NULL);
    ...
}
```
Reason:
When `MSG_PEEK` is used, `__mptcp_recvmsg_mskq` does not remove skbs from the receive queue. The `sk_wait_data` call with `NULL` as the last argument always finds available data (the peeked skbs) in the queue and returns immediately without sleeping. This causes the `while` loop to iterate infinitely without making progress, leading to a soft lockup.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
err = sk_wait_data(sk, &timeo, last);
```
and inside `__mptcp_recvmsg_mskq`:
```c
if (flags & MSG_PEEK) {
    ...
    *last = skb;
    continue;
}
...
} else {
    *last = skb;
}
```
Reason:
By tracking the last peeked skb via the `last` parameter, `sk_wait_data` can determine whether there is new data beyond the last peeked skb. If the last peeked skb is still at the tail of the receive queue, the function will properly wait instead of returning immediately, preventing the infinite loop. The assignments `*last = skb` update the pointer each time a peeked skb is skipped or fully consumed, ensuring accurate waiting decisions.

